ZTEXT001000013db02f9bfc70000000
Zero Studio - original native GUI editor

The editor implementation, source composer, renderer, input handling, file
operations and compiler pipeline are written entirely in Zero. Its 5x7 glyphs
are the original Zero OS glyphs. No GUI toolkit, external editor, browser engine,
font asset, C, assembly, Python or shell program is included in the product.

Native hosts

Linux x86-64: original X11 wire protocol client over a local Unix socket.
DISPLAY=:N and unix:N are accepted. MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 authentication is read
from XAUTHORITY or HOME/.Xauthority. An X11 server or Xwayland is required;
a Wayland-only connection is not implemented. The client validates 24-bit
TrueColor depth, 32-bit pixel padding, byte order and request size. Remote X11,
alternate display screens and unusual pixel formats are not supported yet.

Windows x64: documented Win32 host APIs create a window and transfer the
editor's own pixels. The editor handles its own text, caret and keyboard;
it does not embed an operating-system text editor control. Linux and Windows
share the original renderer and editor logic. The fixed 1280x800 canvas fits
a 1920x1080 desktop. The OS pointer cursor and an insertion caret are visible.

The Windows executable has been exercised through Wine's actual graphical
window/event path. Native Windows hardware has not been tested in this session.

Use

  zero-studio [SOURCE.zero]
  zero-studio [SOURCE.ezero]

Ctrl+O opens a path prompt. Ctrl+S opens the save prompt, prefilled with the
current path. Enter confirms; Escape cancels. F5 builds the in-memory buffer.
Ctrl+Q closes; if there are unsaved changes, press it again to discard them.
An X11 window-manager close request uses the same unsaved-change guard.
Arrows, Home, End, Page Up, Page Down, Backspace, Delete, Tab and Enter work.
Click in the source area to place the caret. Positions start at one.

Open stages and validates the entire source before changing the document.
Missing files, binary input and oversize files leave the document intact.
CRLF/CR line endings become LF in memory; the status reports this conversion.
The disk file changes only after saving. Save writes an exclusive temporary
sibling, flushes it, then atomically replaces the destination. A failed write
or rename preserves the original destination. Existing temporary files are
never removed or reused.

F5 uses the original compiler applications in-process; no subprocess runs.
A .ezero path selects frontend -> package converter -> runtime -> Zero compiler.
Other source paths select the Zero compiler. Output is SOURCE.zrun on Linux
and SOURCE.zrun.exe on Windows. Rebuilds publish atomically after successful
compilation and preserve the previous executable on syntax or write errors.
The editor does not automatically execute programs it compiles.

Portable installation

Keep these original companions in lib/ beside the editor executable:
  zero64.app
  frontend64.app
  pack64.app
  runtime-prefix.zero.part
  runtime.zero.part

These are the same companions distributed with the native Zero/Ezero compiler
commands. Copying the editor and lib/ together permits relocation, including
parent paths containing spaces. The executable itself is generated directly
by the original Zero compiler as native ELF or PE, the host compatibility
formats used by Linux and Windows.

Build

editor/host/editor.zero is the complete compilable source. The original
editor/host/compose.zero composes it reproducibly from the adjacent .zero.part
files. After compiling compose.zero with Zero:

  studio-compose PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_SOURCE.zero
  zero NEW_SOURCE.zero NEW_LINUX_EXECUTABLE --host
  zero NEW_SOURCE.zero NEW_WINDOWS_EXECUTABLE --windows

Existing composition destinations are refused. The native builder can compile
the complete editor.zero directly. Compiler host primitives required are
hostsyscall, hostlibrary, hostsymbol and hostcall12; their implementation is
also original Zero instruction data inside the self-hosted compiler.

Current bounds

One ASCII source document, 65535 bytes; paths entered in the GUI are ASCII and
at most 500 bytes. An overlong startup path is refused instead of truncated.
The compiler diagnostics are bounded status messages: Ezero's frontend error
text is shown; Zero currently reports a syntax failure without a line number.
There is no text selection, clipboard, undo history, IME, Unicode source
editing, debugger, tabbed workspace or language-server protocol yet. Page
navigation uses bounded byte jumps; visual lines wrap at 98 columns.

Verification

Actual Linux Xorg and Windows Wine GUI workflows exercised keyboard input,
mouse placement, save, reopen, missing-file handling and unsaved-close guard.
Both hosts compiled Zero and quote-free Ezero in-process and executed the
generated sample externally in the test harness with the expected output.
An independent original Zero harness checked ten file-preservation, size,
symlink, CRLF, temporary-collision, failed-rename and memory-boundary cases.
External validation tools and screenshots are kept outside the project tree.
